    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Kojiki gathers myths, genealogies, songs and early imperial history into one account that presents the Yamato line as descended from heavenly deities and as rightful heir to the land.</li><li>Its preface says it was written down by Ō no Yasumaro from what Hieda no Are had memorised, and it is usually considered the oldest extant literary work in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</li><li>Neglected in the Heian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> periods, it was raised to the status of a sacred text by the nativist scholars of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> and, above all, by Motoori Norinaga's Kojiki-den.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Motoori Norinaga spent 34 years, from 1764 to 1798, on his 44-volume study of the Kojiki, the Kojiki-den. He valued the Kojiki over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nihon-shoki">Nihon Shoki</a> because it was free of what he called Chinese mentality.</p></aside>
    </section>

